<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1820s–<a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black War was a period of violent conflict between British colonists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanians</a> in Van Diemen's Land from the mid-1820s to about 1832. As settlement spread across the island, Aboriginal attacks on settlers were met by killings and the declaration of martial law in 1828. The 'Black Line' of 1830 failed to round up the Aboriginal people, but by the early 1830s most survivors had been removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flinders-island">Flinders Island</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-war.jpg" alt="Black War" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown British artist, after John Allen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aborigines_attacking_Milton_farm.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>A British penal settlement was established in Van Diemen's Land, now Tasmania, in 1803, when the Aboriginal population was between 3,000 and 7,000 people. Until the 1820s there was only sporadic violence, often caused by competition for game and by the abduction of Aboriginal women and children by settlers. On 3 May 1804 soldiers, settlers and convicts fired on a hunting party of 100 to 300 Aboriginal people at Risdon Cove, and witnesses said that between three and 50 men, women and children died. From 1824 the conflict intensified as Aboriginal warriors resisted the rapid spread of settlement over their land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Aboriginal attacks on colonists more than doubled each year from 1825 to 1828. The warriors fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war, usually attacking by day in parties of 10 to 20 men. On 1 November 1828 Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-arthur">George Arthur</a> declared martial law against the Aboriginal people of the settled districts. It lasted more than three years, the longest period in Australian history, and only one colonist was ever prosecuted for killing an Aboriginal person. In the north-west, shepherds of the Van Diemen's Land Company ambushed up to 30 Aboriginal people at the Cape Grim massacre of 10 February 1828.</p><p>In October 1830 about 2,200 men, soldiers, convict servants and civilians, formed the Black Line, a staggered front more than 300 kilometres long meant to drive four of the nine Aboriginal nations onto the Tasman Peninsula. Bad weather and rough terrain broke the cordon, and it captured two people and killed two before it was disbanded on 26 November. Between 1830 and 1831 George Augustus Robinson and his Aboriginal negotiators, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truganini">Truganini</a>, secured the surrender of the Aboriginal fighters. Martial law was revoked in January 1832.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Almost all the remaining Aboriginal people were removed from mainland Tasmania between 1832 and 1835. About 220 survivors were held at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wybalenna">Wybalenna</a> mission on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flinders-island">Flinders Island</a>, where respiratory disease and a low birth rate reduced them to 46 by 1847, when it closed. The Black War is thought to have killed more than 200 colonists and between 600 and 900 Aboriginal people. Lyndall Ryan estimates 838 killed in eastern Tasmania from 1823 to 1832, and Nicholas Clements puts deaths from frontier violence at about 600, with a possible range of 400 to 1,000.</p><p>Historians disagree about the conflict's character. Keith Windschuttle called it an outbreak of criminality, a view challenged by James Boyce, Henry Reynolds and others. Robert Hughes, Boyce and others describe the near-destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanians</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, while Reynolds, Richard Broome and Clements hold that the colonial authorities did not pursue a policy of destruction.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Black War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>Black War</b> of the 1820s colonists and soldiers killed many Aboriginal Tasmanians, and the survivors were removed to Wybalenna on Flinders Island in the 1830s.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-tasmania">Colony of Tasmania</a></li></ul></section>
